CHAPTERS=[('II/08', 'Differentiability in Several Variables', 'ch08_differentiability_in_several_variables'), ('II/09', 'Inverse and Implicit Function Principles', 'ch09_inverse_and_implicit_function_principles'), ('II/10', 'Riemann Integration', 'ch10_riemann_integration'), ('II/11', 'Pointwise and Uniform Convergence', 'ch11_pointwise_and_uniform_convergence'), ('II/12', 'Interchanging Limits, Derivatives and Integrals', 'ch12_interchanging_limits_derivatives_and_integrals'), ('II/13', 'Infinite Series of Functions', 'ch13_infinite_series_of_functions'), ('II/14', 'Trigonometric Series', 'ch14_trigonometric_series'), ('II/15', 'Pathological and Nowhere-Differentiable Functions', 'ch15_pathological_and_nowhere_differentiable_functions'), ('II/16', 'Contraction Mappings', 'ch16_contraction_mappings'), ('II/17', 'Brouwer-Type Fixed-Point Ideas', 'ch17_brouwer_type_fixed_point_ideas'), ('II/18', 'Integral Equations', 'ch18_integral_equations'), ('II/19', 'Elementary Existence Theory for ODEs', 'ch19_elementary_existence_theory_for_odes'), ('II/20', 'Polynomial Interpolation', 'ch20_polynomial_interpolation'), ('II/21', 'Polynomial Approximation', 'ch21_polynomial_approximation'), ('II/22', 'Chebyshev and Minimax Approximation', 'ch22_chebyshev_and_minimax_approximation'), ('II/23', 'The Alternation Principle', 'ch23_the_alternation_principle'), ('II/24', 'Numerical Quadrature', 'ch24_numerical_quadrature'), ('II/25', 'Continued Fractions and Approximation Topics', 'ch25_continued_fractions_and_approximation_topics')]
DATA={'II/08': {'intro': 'Multivariable differentiability replaces a one-dimensional slope by the best linear approximation. The derivative is therefore an operator, not merely a list of partial derivatives, and the remainder estimate is the central invariant statement.', 'sections': [('Directional and partial derivatives', 'Partial derivatives probe coordinate directions, while directional derivatives probe arbitrary directions; neither collection alone automatically guarantees differentiability.'), ('Fréchet differentiability', 'A map $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is differentiable at $a$ when $f(a+h)=f(a)+Df(a)h+o(\\|h\\|)$.'), ('Jacobian matrices', 'After choosing standard bases, the derivative is represented by the Jacobian matrix.'), ('Chain rule', 'Derivatives compose as linear maps: $D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))Df(a)$.'), ('Gradient and level sets', 'For scalar-valued functions, $Df(a)h=\\nabla f(a)\\cdot h$; gradients are normal to regular level sets.'), ('Higher derivatives', 'Second derivatives assemble into the Hessian, a bilinear form governing second-order behavior.'), ('Taylor approximation', "Taylor's formula organizes linear and quadratic approximations plus a controlled remainder."), ('Critical points', 'At interior extrema differentiable scalar functions have zero gradient; Hessian definiteness gives second-order classification.')], 'theorems': [('Differentiability implies continuity', 'If $f$ is differentiable at $a$, then $f$ is continuous at $a$.', 'Write $f(a+h)-f(a)=Df(a)h+r(h)$ with $\\|r(h)\\|/\\|h\\|\\to0$. The bounded linear term and the remainder both tend to zero.'), ('Chain rule', 'If $f$ is differentiable at $a$ and $g$ at $f(a)$, then $g\\circ f$ is differentiable at $a$ with derivative $Dg(f(a))Df(a)$.', 'Insert the linear-plus-remainder expansions for $f$ and $g$; boundedness of $Dg$ and the smallness of both remainders produce an $o(\\|h\\|)$ total error.'), ('Symmetry of mixed partials', 'If second partial derivatives are continuous near a point, then mixed partials agree there.', 'Apply the one-variable mean-value theorem twice to a rectangular difference quotient and pass to the limit using continuity.')], 'dossiers': [('A Jacobian by linearization', 'For $f(x,y)=(x^2y,e^{x+y})$, compute $Df(1,0)$.', 'The Jacobian is $\\begin{pmatrix}2xy&x^2\\\\e^{x+y}&e^{x+y}\\end{pmatrix}$, hence at $(1,0)$ it is $\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\e&e\\end{pmatrix}$.'), ('Directional derivatives without differentiability', 'Show $f(x,y)=|xy|^{1/2}$ has directional derivatives at the origin but is not differentiable there.', 'Along $(tu,tv)$, $f=|t|\\sqrt{|uv|}$, so one-sided behavior depends linearly only in magnitude and fails a genuine linear approximation; along $y=x$, $f(t,t)=|t|$, which is not $o(\\|(t,t)\\|)$.'), ('Gradient normal to a level set', 'For $F(x,y)=x^2+2y^2$, find the normal direction to $F=3$ at $(1,1)$.', '$\\nabla F=(2x,4y)$, so at $(1,1)$ a normal vector is $(2,4)$.'), ('Chain rule in coordinates', "Let $f(t)=(t,t^2)$ and $g(x,y)=xe^y$. Compute $(g\\circ f)'(1)$ using the chain rule.", '$Df(1)=(1,2)^T$ and $Dg(1,1)=(e,e)$, so the derivative is $e+2e=3e$.'), ('Quadratic approximation', 'Find the Hessian of $f(x,y)=x^2+xy+3y^2$.', '$H=\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\1&6\\end{pmatrix}$, constant everywhere.'), ('Critical-point classification', 'Classify the origin for $f(x,y)=x^2+4y^2$.', 'The gradient vanishes and the Hessian is positive definite, so the origin is a strict local minimum.'), ('A saddle', 'Classify the origin for $f(x,y)=x^2-y^2$.', 'The Hessian has eigenvalues $2$ and $-2$, hence is indefinite; the origin is a saddle.'), ('Differentiability from continuous partials', 'Explain why continuous first partial derivatives near a point imply differentiability there.', 'Move from the point to a nearby point along coordinate segments and apply the one-variable mean-value theorem; continuity of the partials controls the remainder uniformly by $o(\\|h\\|)$.'), ('Derivative of a norm square', 'For $f(x)=\\|x\\|_2^2$ on $\\mathbb R^n$, compute $Df(x)$.', '$Df(x)h=2x\\cdot h$, so the gradient is $2x$.'), ('Derivative of a matrix map', 'For $F(A)=A^2$ on square matrices, compute $DF(A)H$.', 'Expand $(A+H)^2-A^2=AH+HA+H^2$; the linear part is $AH+HA$ and $\\|H^2\\|=o(\\|H\\|)$.'), ('Second-order Taylor formula', 'Write the quadratic Taylor approximation of $e^{x+y}$ at $(0,0)$.', 'Since $e^{x+y}=1+(x+y)+\\frac12(x+y)^2+o(\\|(x,y)\\|^2)$, the quadratic approximation is $1+x+y+\\frac12(x^2+2xy+y^2)$.'), ('Differentiability synthesis', 'Why are partial derivatives not the definition of differentiability?', 'They test only coordinate directions. Differentiability demands one linear map approximate all small directions simultaneously with an error negligible relative to distance.')], 'exercises': [('Compute the gradient of $x^2y+y^3$.', 'Differentiate coordinatewise.', 'It is $(2xy,x^2+3y^2)$.'), ('Find the Jacobian of $(x+y,xy)$.', 'Differentiate each component.', '$\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\y&x\\end{pmatrix}$.'), ('Show linear maps are differentiable.', 'Try the map itself as derivative.', 'For $L$, $L(a+h)-L(a)-Lh=0$.'), ('Find the Hessian of $x^2+xy+y^2$.', 'Differentiate twice.', '$\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{pmatrix}$.'), ('What is $D(f+g)$?', 'Use linearity of the approximation.', '$Df+Dg$.'), ('State the critical-point necessary condition.', 'Assume an interior extremum.', 'The gradient must vanish.'), ('If $Df(a)=0$, is $a$ necessarily an extremum?', 'Think of a cubic.', 'No; $f(x)=x^3$ at $0$ is a counterexample.'), ('Compute $D(x\\mapsto Ax)$.', 'It is linear already.', 'The derivative is the constant map $A$.')]}, 'II/09': {'intro': 'The inverse and implicit function principles are local nonlinear analogues of solving an invertible linear system. This chapter is a fresh canonical construction because the source atlas contains no mapped legacy chapter for II/09.', 'sections': [('Local invertibility', 'A nonlinear map with an invertible derivative should behave like its linearization on a sufficiently small neighborhood.'), ('Inverse function theorem', 'Invertibility of $Df(a)$ gives a local differentiable inverse near $a$.'), ('Derivative of the inverse', 'The inverse derivative is the inverse linear map: $D(f^{-1})(f(a))=Df(a)^{-1}$.'), ('Implicit equations', 'A system $F(x,y)=0$ can be solved locally for $y$ as a function of $x$ when the $y$-Jacobian is invertible.'), ('Implicit differentiation', 'The derivative of the implicit function is obtained by solving the differentiated linear system.'), ('Regular level sets', 'Full-rank derivatives make level sets locally look like Euclidean subspaces.'), ('Contraction proof strategy', 'One proof route reformulates the nonlinear equation as a contraction on a small ball.'), ('Failure modes', 'Singular derivative, noninjectivity, or rank loss can destroy local solvability or uniqueness.')], 'theorems': [('Inverse function theorem', 'If $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^n$ is $C^1$ near $a$ and $Df(a)$ is invertible, then $f$ is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism between neighborhoods of $a$ and $f(a)$.', 'After composing with $Df(a)^{-1}$, reduce to derivative near the identity. On a sufficiently small ball the nonlinear error has Lipschitz constant below one, so solving $f(x)=y$ becomes a contraction problem. Differentiability of the inverse follows from the linearized equation.'), ('Derivative of inverse', 'Under the hypotheses of the inverse theorem, $D(f^{-1})(f(a))=Df(a)^{-1}$.', 'Differentiate $f^{-1}\\circ f=\\mathrm{id}$ and use the chain rule.'), ('Implicit function theorem', 'If $F:\\mathbb R^{n+m}\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is $C^1$, $F(a,b)=0$, and $D_yF(a,b)$ is invertible, then near $(a,b)$ the equation $F(x,y)=0$ has a unique $C^1$ solution $y=g(x)$.', 'Apply the inverse function theorem to $\\Phi(x,y)=(x,F(x,y))$, whose derivative is block triangular with invertible diagonal blocks.')], 'dossiers': [('One-dimensional inverse theorem', "Explain why $f'(a)\\ne0$ yields local invertibility for a $C^1$ real function.", "Continuity keeps $f'$ of one sign near $a$, so $f$ is strictly monotone there. The inverse exists locally and differentiating $f(f^{-1}(y))=y$ gives the inverse derivative."), ('A locally invertible map', 'For $f(x,y)=(e^x\\cos y,e^x\\sin y)$, determine where the derivative is invertible.', 'The Jacobian determinant is $e^{2x}$, always positive, so the derivative is invertible everywhere; global injectivity fails because of $2\\pi$-periodicity in $y$.'), ('Local versus global inverse', 'Why does the inverse function theorem not imply a global inverse for the previous map?', 'The theorem is local. Distinct points differing by $(0,2\\pi k)$ have the same image despite nonsingular derivative everywhere.'), ('Implicit circle graph', "Near $(0,1)$ solve $x^2+y^2=1$ for $y$ and compute $y'(0)$.", "Take $F=x^2+y^2-1$; $F_y=2y=2$ at $(0,1)$, so $y=\\sqrt{1-x^2}$ locally and $y'=-x/y$, hence $y'(0)=0$."), ('Implicit derivative formula', 'Derive $Dg(a)=-D_yF(a,b)^{-1}D_xF(a,b)$.', 'Differentiate $F(x,g(x))=0$: $D_xF+D_yF\\,Dg=0$, then solve for $Dg$.'), ('Regular level curve', 'Show $x^2+y^2=1$ is locally a smooth curve at every point.', 'The gradient $(2x,2y)$ never vanishes on the unit circle, so one partial derivative is nonzero and the implicit theorem applies.'), ('A singular failure', 'What happens to $F(x,y)=y^2-x^3=0$ at the origin?', '$D_yF(0,0)=0$, so the theorem does not apply. The cusp cannot be represented as a single smooth function $y=g(x)$ through the origin.'), ('Coordinate straightening', 'Explain geometrically what the implicit theorem says about a regular level set.', 'After a local $C^1$ change of coordinates, the equation becomes the coordinate condition $y=0$, so the level set is locally flat.'), ('Newton linearization link', "Relate inverse-theorem solvability to Newton's method.", 'Both use invertibility of the derivative to solve a linearized correction equation. The theorem supplies local uniqueness and stability; Newton iterates attempt to realize that correction computationally.'), ('Parameter dependence', 'If $F(x,y)=y^3+y-x$, show $y$ is a smooth local function of $x$ everywhere on the solution set.', '$F_y=3y^2+1>0$ everywhere, so the implicit theorem applies at every solution point.'), ('Rank perspective', 'Why is the determinant condition coordinate-dependent notation for an invariant statement?', 'Nonzero determinant means the derivative linear map is an isomorphism. Under basis changes the determinant is multiplied by nonzero factors, so invertibility itself is invariant.'), ('IFT synthesis', 'Summarize inverse and implicit theorems as nonlinear linear algebra.', 'An invertible derivative lets one solve all variables locally; an invertible derivative block lets one solve selected variables locally. Both are perturbative versions of solving nonsingular linear systems.')], 'exercises': [('Can $x\\mapsto x^3$ use the inverse theorem at $0$?', 'Check the derivative.', "No; $f'(0)=0$, although a continuous inverse exists globally."), ('Find the inverse derivative formula in one dimension.', 'Differentiate an identity.', "$(f^{-1})'(f(a))=1/f'(a)$."), ('For $F(x,y)=x+y+xy$, compute $F_y(0,0)$.', 'Differentiate in $y$.', 'It is $1$, so $y$ is locally a function of $x$.'), ('Solve the previous equation explicitly.', 'Factor in $y$.', '$y=-x/(1+x)$ near $0$.'), ('Why does nonsingular Jacobian imply local openness?', 'Use local diffeomorphism.', 'A local diffeomorphism maps neighborhoods to neighborhoods.'), ('State a failure of global injectivity with nonsingular derivative.', 'Use periodicity.', 'The exponential-polar map is a standard example.'), ('What derivative block matters for solving $F(x,y)=0$ for $y$?', 'Differentiate with respect to the solved variables.', '$D_yF$.'), ('What is a regular value condition?', 'Think surjectivity.', 'At every point of the level set, the derivative is surjective.')]}, 'II/10': {'intro': 'Riemann integration quantifies accumulation by controlled finite sums. The theory rests on upper and lower sums, oscillation, and the principle that sufficiently fine partitions suppress local variation.', 'sections': [('Partitions and sums', 'Tagged partitions produce Riemann sums; upper and lower sums eliminate dependence on tags.'), ('Riemann integrability', 'A bounded function is integrable when upper and lower integrals coincide.'), ('Darboux criterion', 'Integrability is equivalent to making the gap between upper and lower sums arbitrarily small.'), ('Continuous functions', 'Uniform continuity on a compact interval makes continuous functions Riemann integrable.'), ('Monotone functions', 'Monotonicity controls total oscillation and also guarantees integrability.'), ('Algebra of integrable functions', 'Sums, scalar multiples, products, and absolute values preserve integrability under standard hypotheses.'), ('Fundamental theorem', 'Integration and differentiation are inverse operations for continuous integrands.'), ('Improper limits preview', 'Classical Riemann integration on compact intervals is the base case for later extension to unbounded domains or singular integrands.')], 'theorems': [('Darboux criterion', 'A bounded function on $[a,b]$ is Riemann integrable iff for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is a partition $P$ with $U(f,P)-L(f,P)<\\varepsilon$.', 'One direction is immediate from equality of upper and lower integrals. Conversely a partition with small gap traps all finer upper and lower sums within epsilon, forcing the two integrals to coincide.'), ('Continuous integrability', 'Every continuous function on a compact interval is Riemann integrable.', 'Uniform continuity makes oscillation on sufficiently short subintervals uniformly small; summing oscillation times interval length controls the Darboux gap.'), ('Fundamental theorem, Part I', "If $f$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and $F(x)=\\int_a^x f(t)dt$, then $F'(x)=f(x)$.", 'The difference quotient is an average value of $f$ over a shrinking interval; continuity makes that average converge to $f(x)$.')], 'dossiers': [('Integral of a step function', 'Compute the integral of $f=2$ on $[0,1)$ and $f=5$ on $[1,3]$.', 'The integral is area sum $2\\cdot1+5\\cdot2=12$.'), ('Darboux gap estimate', 'If oscillation of $f$ on every partition interval is at most $\\eta$, bound $U-L$.', 'The gap is at most $\\eta\\sum\\Delta x_i=\\eta(b-a)$.'), ('Continuous integrability mechanism', 'Explain where compactness enters the proof.', 'Continuity on $[a,b]$ becomes uniform continuity, so one partition scale works simultaneously across the whole interval.'), ('Monotone integrability', 'Show an increasing bounded function on $[a,b]$ is integrable.', 'For an equal partition into $n$ pieces, the upper-lower gap telescopes to $(b-a)(f(b)-f(a))/n$.'), ('Integral of $x$', 'Compute $\\int_0^1x\\,dx$ from equal Riemann sums.', 'Right-endpoint sums are $n^{-2}\\sum_{k=1}^nk=(n+1)/(2n)\\to1/2$.'), ('A single discontinuity', 'Why is a bounded function with one jump discontinuity still Riemann integrable?', 'Isolate the jump in an interval of arbitrarily small length; on the remaining compact pieces continuity or small oscillation controls the Darboux gap.'), ('Dirichlet function', 'Why is $1_{\\mathbb Q}$ not Riemann integrable on any nontrivial interval?', 'Every subinterval contains rationals and irrationals, so every upper sum is the interval length and every lower sum is zero.'), ('Absolute-value inequality', 'Prove $|\\int f|\\le\\int|f|$ for Riemann-integrable $f$.', 'Pointwise $-|f|\\le f\\le|f|$; monotonicity of the integral gives the estimate.'), ('Mean value for integrals', 'If $f$ is continuous on $[a,b]$, show $\\int_a^bf=f(c)(b-a)$ for some $c$.', 'The integral average lies between the minimum and maximum of $f$; the intermediate-value theorem gives a point attaining that average.'), ('FTC computation', 'Evaluate $\\int_0^1 3x^2\\,dx$.', 'An antiderivative is $x^3$, so the integral is $1$.'), ('Additivity', 'Explain why $\\int_a^cf+\\int_c^bf=\\int_a^bf$.', 'Combine partitions on the two subintervals or compare Riemann sums; the common endpoint has no effect on the sum.'), ('Integration synthesis', 'Why is Riemann integration a limit of finite-dimensional data?', 'Each sum uses finitely many sample values and interval lengths; integrability asserts that all sufficiently fine such finite approximations converge to one common number.')], 'exercises': [('Compute $\\int_0^2 1\\,dx$.', 'Area of a rectangle.', 'It is $2$.'), ('Compute $\\int_0^1x^2\\,dx$.', 'Use FTC.', 'It is $1/3$.'), ('Is every continuous function on $[a,b]$ integrable?', 'Use compactness.', 'Yes.'), ('Is every bounded function integrable?', "Recall Dirichlet's example.", 'No.'), ('State additivity over adjacent intervals.', 'Split at $c$.', '$\\int_a^b=\\int_a^c+\\int_c^b$.'), ('If $f\\ge0$, what can be said about the integral?', 'Use monotonicity.', 'The integral is nonnegative.'), ('Does changing one function value change the Riemann integral?', 'Think of partition sums.', 'No, for an integrable function a finite modification does not change the integral.'), ('What is $\\int_a^a f$?', 'Zero interval length.', 'It is $0$.')]}, 'II/11': {'intro': 'Pointwise convergence controls each input separately; uniform convergence controls all inputs at one common scale. This distinction determines which global properties survive passage to limits.', 'sections': [('Pointwise convergence', 'For each fixed $x$, the numerical sequence $f_n(x)$ converges.'), ('Uniform convergence', 'One index $N$ works for every $x$ simultaneously.'), ('Supremum norm', 'Uniform convergence is exactly convergence in the sup norm when that norm is finite.'), ('Continuity under uniform limits', 'Uniform limits of continuous functions are continuous.'), ('Cauchy criterion', 'Uniform convergence is equivalent to a uniform Cauchy condition in complete codomains.'), ('Examples of failure', 'Pointwise limits can lose continuity or exchange of limits.'), ('Uniform approximation', 'Uniform error bounds quantify global approximation quality.'), ('Compact-domain tests', 'Monotonicity and compactness can sometimes upgrade pointwise convergence to uniform convergence.')], 'theorems': [('Uniform limit theorem', 'A uniform limit of continuous functions is continuous.', 'Choose one approximant uniformly close to the limit and use its continuity to bridge values of the limit at nearby points.'), ('Uniform Cauchy criterion', 'For complete codomain $Y$, a sequence $f_n:X\\to Y$ converges uniformly iff for every $\\varepsilon$ the tail is uniformly epsilon-Cauchy.', 'Uniform convergence implies uniform Cauchy by the triangle inequality. Conversely each pointwise tail is Cauchy and converges; the same uniform tail estimate survives in the limit.'), ('Dini-type principle', 'If continuous $f_n$ on compact $K$ decrease pointwise to continuous $f$, then convergence is uniform.', 'The open sets where $f_n-f<\\varepsilon$ increase and cover compact $K$; a finite subcover reduces to one sufficiently large index.')], 'dossiers': [('Powers on $[0,1]$', 'Analyze $f_n(x)=x^n$ pointwise and uniformly.', 'Pointwise the limit is $0$ on $[0,1)$ and $1$ at $1$, discontinuous. Uniform convergence fails because $\\sup|f_n-f|=1$.'), ('Powers on $[0,a]$', 'For $0<a<1$, show $x^n\\to0$ uniformly.', '$\\sup_{[0,a]}x^n=a^n\\to0$.'), ('Uniform implies pointwise', 'Prove the implication directly.', 'A uniform epsilon index works in particular at any fixed point.'), ('Continuity of the uniform limit', 'Give the three-term epsilon estimate.', '$|f(x)-f(a)|\\le|f-f_N|(x)+|f_N(x)-f_N(a)|+|f_N-f|(a)$, and choose the three terms below thirds of epsilon.'), ('Sup-norm reformulation', 'Show $f_n\\to f$ uniformly iff $\\|f_n-f\\|_\\infty\\to0$.', 'This is just the definition of supremum norm and uniform epsilon control.'), ('Nonuniform pointwise convergence', 'Analyze $f_n(x)=x/(1+nx)$ on $[0,1]$.', 'For each fixed $x$, the limit is $0$. The maximum occurs near the endpoint and is $1/(n+1)$, so in fact convergence is uniform; this example illustrates the need to compute the supremum rather than guess.'), ('A genuine nonuniform sequence', 'Analyze $f_n(x)=nx(1-x^2)^n$ on $[0,1]$.', 'For fixed $x>0$ the exponential factor wins and the limit is zero, also at $0$. But maxima remain of order $\\sqrt n$, so convergence is not uniform.'), ('Uniform Cauchy estimate', 'If $\\|f_{n+1}-f_n\\|_\\infty\\le2^{-n}$, prove uniform convergence in $C(K)$.', 'The tail is bounded by a geometric series, hence uniformly Cauchy; completeness of the sup-norm space gives uniform convergence.'), ('Uniform approximation preserves boundedness', 'If $f_n$ are uniformly close to bounded $f_N$, show the limit is bounded.', 'For one large $N$, $|f|\\le|f-f_N|+|f_N|$, and the uniform error is globally bounded.'), ('Interchanging an outer limit', 'If $x_m\\to x$ and $f_n\\to f$ uniformly with continuous $f_n$, explain why $f(x_m)\\to f(x)$.', 'Uniform convergence gives continuity of $f$, then ordinary continuity handles the sequence $x_m$.'), ('Dini mechanism', "Why is compactness decisive in Dini's theorem?", 'Pointwise convergence gives one local index per point; compactness extracts finitely many local neighborhoods and hence one maximum index working globally.'), ('Convergence synthesis', 'Why is uniform convergence the natural topology for preserving continuity?', 'Continuity is a local statement needing one approximation to work at two nearby points simultaneously. Uniform error control supplies that common approximant everywhere.')], 'exercises': [('Does uniform convergence imply pointwise?', 'Use definitions.', 'Yes.'), ('Does pointwise imply uniform?', 'Use $x^n$ on $[0,1]$.', 'No.'), ('If $\\|f_n-f\\|_\\infty\\le1/n$, what follows?', 'Take the bound to zero.', 'Uniform convergence.'), ('Can a uniform limit of continuous functions be discontinuous?', 'Use the uniform limit theorem.', 'No.'), ('Find the pointwise limit of $x/n$.', 'Fix $x$.', 'It is $0$.'), ('Is $x/n\\to0$ uniform on $[0,1]$?', 'Take the supremum.', 'Yes.'), ('Is $x/n\\to0$ uniform on $\\mathbb R$?', 'Supremum is infinite.', 'No.'), ('State the uniform Cauchy condition.', 'One index for all points and tail pairs.', 'For every epsilon, $d(f_m(x),f_n(x))<\\varepsilon$ for all $x$ and all large $m,n$.')]}, 'II/12': {'intro': 'Passing a limit through a derivative or integral requires more than pointwise convergence. The correct hypotheses combine uniform control with convergence of derivative data or domination of integral errors.', 'sections': [('Limits and integrals', 'Uniform convergence allows Riemann integration to commute with limits on compact intervals.'), ('Integral error estimates', 'The difference of integrals is controlled by interval length times the sup-norm error.'), ('Limits and derivatives', 'Uniform convergence of derivatives plus convergence at one base point yields a differentiable limit.'), ('Why derivative exchange is harder', 'Differentiation amplifies small-scale oscillation and therefore needs stronger hypotheses.'), ('Series versions', 'The same principles apply to term-by-term integration and differentiation of function series.'), ('Uniform convergence on compacta', 'Local uniform convergence is often the right compromise on noncompact domains.'), ('Counterexamples', 'Pointwise convergence alone can fail spectacularly for derivatives and integrals.'), ('Stability viewpoint', 'Interchange theorems are continuity statements for integral or derivative operators in suitable function-space topologies.')], 'theorems': [('Uniform limit under the integral', 'If $f_n\\to f$ uniformly on $[a,b]$ and each $f_n$ is Riemann integrable, then $f$ is integrable and $\\int f_n\\to\\int f$.', 'The integral difference is at most $(b-a)\\|f_n-f\\|_\\infty$, which tends to zero; integrability follows by approximating $f$ uniformly with an integrable $f_n$.'), ('Derivative limit theorem', "If $f_n\\in C^1[a,b]$, $f_n(x_0)$ converges at one point, and $f_n'$ converges uniformly to $g$, then $f_n$ converges uniformly to a differentiable $f$ with $f'=g$.", "Write $f_n(x)=f_n(x_0)+\\int_{x_0}^x f_n'$. Uniform convergence of derivatives gives uniform Cauchy control of the integrals, and the fundamental theorem identifies the derivative of the limit."), ('Termwise integration', 'If $\\sum f_n$ converges uniformly and each $f_n$ is integrable, then the sum is integrable and $\\int\\sum f_n=\\sum\\int f_n$.', 'Apply the uniform-limit integral theorem to the partial sums.')], 'dossiers': [('Integral interchange estimate', 'Prove $|\\int_a^b f_n-\\int_a^b f|\\le(b-a)\\|f_n-f\\|_\\infty$.', 'Apply $|\\int g|\\le\\int|g|$ and bound the integrand by its supremum.'), ('A pointwise failure for integrals', 'Construct continuous spikes $f_n$ on $[0,1]$ converging pointwise to $0$ but with integrals not tending to $0$.', 'Use triangular spikes of height $n$ and width $2/n$ centered near zero so area stays $1$ while each fixed positive point eventually lies outside the support.'), ('Termwise integration of a geometric series', 'For $|x|\\le r<1$, integrate $\\sum_{n\\ge0}x^n$ termwise.', 'The series is uniformly convergent by the M-test, so $\\int_0^r\\sum x^n dx=\\sum r^{n+1}/(n+1)=-\\log(1-r)$.'), ('Derivative exchange mechanism', 'Why is convergence at one base point needed?', 'Derivatives determine functions only up to additive constants. Convergence at one point fixes those constants.'), ('Derivative counterexample', "Let $f_n(x)=\\sin(nx)/n$. What happens to $f_n$ and $f_n'$?", "$f_n\\to0$ uniformly, but $f_n'=\\cos(nx)$ does not converge pointwise at most points, showing uniform convergence of functions alone does not control derivatives."), ('Uniform derivative convergence', "If $\\|f_n'-g\\|_\\infty\\to0$, estimate the derivative-integral error.", "For any $x$, $|\\int_{x_0}^x(f_n'-g)|\\le |b-a|\\|f_n'-g\\|_\\infty$."), ('Series of derivatives', "Suppose $\\sum f_n'$ converges uniformly and $\\sum f_n(x_0)$ converges. What follows?", "The series $\\sum f_n$ converges uniformly to a differentiable function whose derivative is $\\sum f_n'$."), ('Local uniform convergence', 'Why is uniform convergence on every compact interval enough for local integral interchange?', 'Any fixed finite integration interval is compact, so the uniform theorem applies there.'), ('Continuity of integration operator', 'View $I(f)=\\int_a^bf$. What is its operator norm on $C[a,b]$ with sup norm?', '$|I(f)|\\le(b-a)\\|f\\|_\\infty$, with equality for the constant function $1$, so the norm is $b-a$.'), ('Differentiation is not bounded in sup norm', 'Use $f_n(x)=\\sin(nx)/n$ to explain.', "$\\|f_n\\|_\\infty=1/n\\to0$ while $\\|f_n'\\|_\\infty=1$, so no fixed bound $\\|f'\\|_\\infty\\le C\\|f\\|_\\infty$ can hold."), ('M-test bridge', 'Why does the Weierstrass M-test often precede termwise integration?', 'It supplies uniform convergence of the function series, exactly the hypothesis needed to exchange sum and integral.'), ('Interchange synthesis', 'What unifies limit/integral and limit/derivative theorems?', 'Both ask whether an operator is continuous under a chosen function-space topology. Integration is continuous in sup norm; differentiation requires a stronger topology controlling derivatives.')], 'exercises': [('If $f_n\\to f$ uniformly, do integrals converge?', 'On a compact interval, yes.', 'Yes, for Riemann-integrable terms.'), ('Bound the integral error by sup error.', 'Use interval length.', 'At most $(b-a)\\|f_n-f\\|_\\infty$.'), ('Does uniform convergence imply derivative convergence?', 'Use $\\sin(nx)/n$.', 'No.'), ('Why one base point in derivative theorem?', 'Constants.', 'Derivative data omit additive constants.'), ('Can a uniformly convergent series be integrated termwise?', 'Under integrability.', 'Yes.'), ('What topology makes integration continuous?', 'Sup norm on a compact interval.', 'The sup norm.'), ('Is differentiation bounded in the sup norm?', 'Use high-frequency functions.', 'No.'), ('If derivatives converge uniformly to $g$, what candidate derivative has the limit?', 'Use the theorem.', '$g$.')]}}
